      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bryson takes its name from Henry Bryson, who built the town’s first log‑cabin home in the late 1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryson%2C_Texas",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first school opened in 1875 in a log cabin called Cottonwood, then became Bryson Academy in 1885 with two new teachers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryson%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the fall of 1902 the Chicago, Rock Island and Texas Railway brought trains to Bryson, linking it to the wider rail network.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryson%2C_Texas",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lake Bryson, a 2.5‑mile‑wide reservoir just northwest of town, supplies water and offers a quiet spot for locals.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
